---
const stacks = [
  {
    name: "Hoplite",
    className: "stack-card stack-card--hoplite",
    shape: "Nginx + embedded Hara",
    services: "1 server lifecycle",
    artifact: "1 executable or OCI image",
    included: "Routing, auth, management, OpenAPI",
    tradeoff: "Young ecosystem; pre-1.0 interfaces",
  },
  {
    name: "Java",
    className: "stack-card",
    shape: "Nginx + JVM application",
    services: "2 server lifecycles",
    artifact: "Proxy config + JAR or image",
    included: "Broad framework ecosystem",
    tradeoff: "Larger runtime envelope and integration surface",
  },
  {
    name: "Python",
    className: "stack-card",
    shape: "Nginx + app server + workers",
    services: "2+ lifecycles and worker pool",
    artifact: "Proxy config + environment or image",
    included: "Fast iteration and broad libraries",
    tradeoff: "Worker sizing and process coordination",
  },
  {
    name: "Lua / OpenResty",
    className: "stack-card",
    shape: "Nginx/OpenResty + Lua modules",
    services: "1 server lifecycle",
    artifact: "Server image + Lua modules",
    included: "Lean request-boundary scripting",
    tradeoff: "Application conventions assembled separately",
  },
];
---
<section class="home-section stack-comparison">
  <header>
    <p class="technical-kicker">03 · Cost / benefit</p>
    <h2>Same host. Less stack to operate.</h2>
    <p>
      A 2 vCPU, 2 GB machine costs the same whichever runtime you choose. The useful
      comparison is how much of that machine—and how much operator time—the stack consumes.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="stack-grid">
    {stacks.map((stack) => (
      <article class={stack.className}>
        <h3>{stack.name}</h3>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Production shape</dt><dd>{stack.shape}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Service surfaces</dt><dd>{stack.services}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Deployable units</dt><dd>{stack.artifact}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>What you gain</dt><dd>{stack.included}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>What you carry</dt><dd>{stack.tradeoff}</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="comparison-conclusion">
    The Hoplite benefit case is not a magically cheaper VM. It is more of the same VM
    available to requests, fewer runtime boundaries, and fewer systems to configure,
    secure, deploy, and observe.
  </p>
</section>
